I'd be more useful as an adviser if I'd made more progress,
but as a general set of guidelines, and only to the middle
game since I've not gotten much past that myself:

1) Obsess on AC and HP to the exclusion of XP.  With a pet
or an altar to help, mines is an endless source of armor to
haul away if needed and to try on; eventually (a very long
eventuality) you can make all of your armor kit +4 or +5
just by stuff you pick up and buc-ID for safe wearing, you
won't need to enchant anything.

2) Sac for _any_ named weapon if you find a coaligned altar,
think seriously about taking the risk to convert even an
attended one if that's the only one you can find.

3) Nurses and foocubi are your friends.

By the time I get to questing, my HP are more like 150 to
200, and AC down to -12 (still pretty wimpy) or better;
that suffices for the easier quests (though my monks keep
getting slaughtered on theirs; monk AC isn't so easy to
create at least for me, but then I've never managed to
buy protection from a priest, only get it by sacs to my
deity) into "hell", which I wade through the early levels
of fairly comfortably, but not beyond, where I start
getting toasted.

4) Big strong pets help a lot too, as does taking the trouble
to ferry several from level to level; keeping out of the way of
Fido or Puss so they can build up their own hit points is
an acquired skill; take some character that finds or starts
with a stethescope, and monitor your pets closely to see how
their HP gain and level gain works as you move through the
early levels.

5) Invisibility and displacement are wonderful ways to cut
down the frequency at which monsters succeed in hitting you
per try, though invisibility, until the spoiler I just this
past week read, makes shopping the pits and shoplifting
preferred.

Once you can get that far (past the quest and started in
"hell" (I can't spell the real name)), seek better advice, as
all this may be just backwards for surviving further than I
am.

